topics.description:
Promises compose well, so most async utilities are tiny.

### sleep
```js
const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));
```

### timeout
Race the work against a timer: `Promise.race([work, new Promise((_, rej) => setTimeout(() => rej(new Error('timeout')), ms))])`. The loser keeps running; use `AbortController` to actually cancel network work. Always clear the timer to avoid keeping Node alive.

### retry with backoff
Loop `for (attempt...)`, `await` the call in `try`, and `sleep(base * 2 ** attempt)` in `catch`. Add random jitter in real systems and retry only on retryable errors.

### Concurrency limit
`Promise.all(items.map(fn))` starts everything at once. To limit to N at a time, create N worker loops that pull from a shared queue/index:

```js
async function pool(items, limit, fn) {
  const results = []; let i = 0;
  await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: limit }, async () => {
    while (i < items.length) { const idx = i++; results[idx] = await fn(items[idx]); }
  }));
  return results;
}
```

### Sequential vs parallel
- Sequential: `for (const x of xs) await f(x)`.
- Parallel: `await Promise.all(xs.map(f))`.
- `reduce` chaining is the same as sequential but harder to read.

### Safe JSON parse
`const safeParse = (s, fallback = null) => { try { return JSON.parse(s); } catch { return fallback; } }`.

### Other
- `Promise.allSettled` collects every result without short-circuiting.
- `Promise.withResolvers()` exposes `resolve`/`reject` outside the executor.
- Wrap callbacks: `util.promisify` in Node or a manual `new Promise`.

